{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794521292,"nonce":"OKlEi1785t8+t//RQYo61Tihukg=","message":"Closing. The two points left open are settled by the design rather than missing: (1) stepped is for computation split into slices, and Poll::Pending from a step means more to do now; work that waits on something is an async block given to spawn, which since the waking change parks on the cluster (Suspension::Pending plus Cluster::wake) or is skipped on the single-thread queue until woken, using no CPU. A step that needs to wait is written as async instead. (2) Calling a Haxe closure from a task is excluded by the boundary rule that native code never calls a host function to report a result or keeps one alive; a task's output reaches Haxe as its future's, stream's or signal's value. docs/design.md now states both under Running work (f7cf62761a).","files":null}]}